WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.828 --> 00:00:10.769
This film is the story of a timid and solitary young man who became a planetary hero.

00:00:13.769 --> 00:00:15.591
How did he succeed in his exploit?

00:00:16.591 --> 00:00:17.390
And afterwards,

00:00:17.731 --> 00:00:22.593
how was his life transformed by fame and by the tragedy of his son's murder?

00:00:23.733 --> 00:00:26.713
By the crowd's adoration and then their rejection.

00:00:28.774 --> 00:00:30.574
The undesirable people he frequented,

00:00:31.414 --> 00:00:34.216
his redemption and his secret life.

00:00:35.392 --> 00:00:38.734
This is the story of Lindbergh in color.

00:00:45.597 --> 00:00:46.779
Nearly a century ago,

00:00:47.219 --> 00:00:49.480
flying alone over the middle of the Atlantic,

00:00:50.021 --> 00:00:55.004
a young American pilot attempts the first non-stop flight from New York to Paris.

00:00:57.025 --> 00:00:59.026
A crazy undertaking at the time.

00:01:18.757 --> 00:01:21.340
In the United States at the end of the First World War,

00:01:21.819 --> 00:01:25.903
all sorts of desperate people are willing to risk their lives for a few dollars.

00:01:31.096 --> 00:01:32.918
Flying over farms and fields,

00:01:33.298 --> 00:01:34.239
barnstormers,

00:01:34.278 --> 00:01:35.960
the first flying circus pilots,

00:01:36.341 --> 00:01:42.706
defy danger to awe their rural audience and convince customers to pay a few dollars for a baptismal flight.

00:01:45.289 --> 00:01:46.611
These flying crazies,

00:01:46.751 --> 00:01:47.912
Charles Lindbergh,

00:01:48.031 --> 00:01:48.972
20 years old,

00:01:49.153 --> 00:01:50.114
nicknamed Slim,

00:01:50.634 --> 00:01:53.377
6 foot 5 inches and weighing only 148 pounds,

00:01:53.456 --> 00:01:55.478
sometimes walks between the wings.

00:01:57.512 --> 00:01:58.272
Even riskier,

00:01:58.472 --> 00:02:00.834
he makes a few dollars by testing new parachutes.

00:02:03.436 --> 00:02:04.298
On his first jump,

00:02:04.618 --> 00:02:05.739
his parachute opens,

00:02:05.879 --> 00:02:07.620
but only at the last moment.

00:02:10.183 --> 00:02:11.664
Baptized Lucky Lindy,

00:02:12.325 --> 00:02:17.750
he makes enough money to buy a World War I surplus airplane for only $500.

00:02:19.111 --> 00:02:21.153
And he becomes a daredevil.

00:02:28.793 --> 00:02:29.372
He says,

00:02:30.493 --> 00:02:33.174
it's where immortality is touched through danger,

00:02:34.495 --> 00:02:36.936
where life meets death on equal plane,

00:02:38.075 --> 00:02:44.117
where man is more than a man and existence both supreme and valueless at the same time.

00:02:45.739 --> 00:02:48.840
Who else but a pioneer of the West could say such a thing?

00:02:52.103 --> 00:02:53.925
Lindbergh was born February 4th,

00:02:53.944 --> 00:02:56.004
1902 in Detroit,

00:02:56.085 --> 00:02:56.504
Michigan.

00:02:57.626 --> 00:02:59.265
His mother was a chemistry teacher.

00:02:59.945 --> 00:03:02.187
She treated him as if he were one of her students.

00:03:02.526 --> 00:03:06.747
She was distant and rigid and thus raised a very introverted boy.

00:03:07.788 --> 00:03:14.030
He grew up along the banks of the Mississippi in a falsely idyllic atmosphere as his parents no longer got along.

00:03:15.751 --> 00:03:16.890
A solitary child,

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:18.951
his only friend is his dog,

00:03:19.171 --> 00:03:19.532
Spot.

00:03:26.224 --> 00:03:30.128
His father was a very severe man and didn't show him affection either.

00:03:31.669 --> 00:03:33.051
A conservative politician,

00:03:33.631 --> 00:03:36.394
he was elected by an illiterate rural constituency.

00:03:40.037 --> 00:03:42.920
At an age when he should have been out playing with other children,

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:47.144
his father was dragging him to the House of Representatives in Washington.

00:03:49.165 --> 00:03:49.807
But it's here,

00:03:50.106 --> 00:03:50.888
in Washington.

00:03:51.436 --> 00:03:55.836
that he saw the Wright brothers take off in the first plane ever to fly.

00:03:55.837 --> 00:04:00.218
1918,

00:04:00.418 --> 00:04:01.698
his father left his wife,

00:04:02.758 --> 00:04:03.179
leaving his

00:04:03.639 --> 00:04:05.240
16-year-old son to run the farm.

00:04:06.719 --> 00:04:12.421
Charles sold the farm materials and livestock to pay for a motorcycle and classes at the university.

00:04:13.561 --> 00:04:15.162
He wanted to become an engineer,

00:04:16.142 --> 00:04:17.584
but he got bored at school.

00:04:19.772 --> 00:04:25.134
Lindbergh was expelled from the university and persuaded his parents to pay for aviation lessons.

00:04:27.394 --> 00:04:27.735
April 8th,

00:04:27.736 --> 00:04:29.095
1922.

00:04:29.695 --> 00:04:31.576
Lindbergh flew for the first time.

00:04:33.596 --> 00:04:35.277
An unforgettable sensation,

00:04:35.997 --> 00:04:39.378
like escaping the terrestrial world which was often frightening to him.

00:04:40.319 --> 00:04:42.679
That day was truly his second birth.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:45.540
He said,

00:04:46.521 --> 00:04:49.525
it was a love of the air and sky and flying,

00:04:49.887 --> 00:04:51.147
the lure of adventure,

00:04:51.548 --> 00:04:53.050
the appreciation of beauty.

00:04:53.692 --> 00:04:55.774
It lay beyond the descriptive words of men.

00:04:57.376 --> 00:05:00.000
This is how Lindbergh went on to become a barnstorm.

00:05:00.761 --> 00:05:01.942
An aerial acrobat.

00:05:05.785 --> 00:05:08.567
He learns to get out of the most perilous situations.

00:05:10.228 --> 00:05:13.632
He wants to progress and fly even more powerful machines.

00:05:16.814 --> 00:05:19.476
He signs up to become an Army fighter pilot.

00:05:21.858 --> 00:05:25.721
He's passionate about formation flying and fighter pilot acrobatics.

00:05:29.348 --> 00:05:33.050
But he can't sign on for longer because the Army's cutting back on troops.

00:05:34.271 --> 00:05:35.851
So in 1925,

00:05:35.853 --> 00:05:37.173
Lieutenant Lindbergh is assigned,

00:05:37.233 --> 00:05:38.012
with some regret,

00:05:38.332 --> 00:05:40.654
to the Army Reserves with the rank of Captain.

00:05:44.516 --> 00:05:47.298
He enters the Post Office's Aerial Delivery Service.

00:05:47.918 --> 00:05:49.120
They're in need of experienced,

00:05:49.139 --> 00:05:49.659
precise,

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:50.860
disciplined pilots.

00:05:55.463 --> 00:05:56.084
For one year...

00:05:56.431 --> 00:05:57.552
Flying day and night,

00:05:57.873 --> 00:06:00.156
Lindbergh transports thousands of letters.

00:06:01.137 --> 00:06:05.521
He accumulates hundreds of hours of flying time and develops great endurance.

00:06:07.163 --> 00:06:09.425
This type of training gives him time to dream.

00:06:10.806 --> 00:06:13.370
He's talented and he gains experience.

00:06:15.915 --> 00:06:17.016
Planes are improving.

00:06:17.576 --> 00:06:19.299
They can now fly longer distances.

00:06:20.199 --> 00:06:22.100
Why not attempt a new distance record?

00:06:23.901 --> 00:06:26.984
Why not try a shot at the Orteg Prize?

00:06:30.045 --> 00:06:30.966
Raymond Orteg,

00:06:31.287 --> 00:06:33.028
a Frenchman who became rich in the U.S.,

00:06:33.129 --> 00:06:35.530
created a prize of $25,000,

00:06:36.031 --> 00:06:37.831
$270,000 today,

00:06:38.292 --> 00:06:41.975
for the first aviator to fly non-stop from New York to Paris.

00:06:43.671 --> 00:06:45.992
He wanted to prolong the wartime alliance,

00:06:46.053 --> 00:06:47.313
the spirit of Lafayette,

00:06:47.553 --> 00:06:49.955
the friendship between France and the U.S.

00:06:56.399 --> 00:07:01.542
24-year-old Charles Augustus Lindbergh decides to throw himself into the race.

00:07:10.232 --> 00:07:12.293
Lindbergh's taught many important St.

00:07:12.353 --> 00:07:13.814
Louis businessmen to fly.

00:07:14.635 --> 00:07:15.535
He contacts them.

00:07:17.717 --> 00:07:18.657
But more importantly,

00:07:19.098 --> 00:07:20.959
he becomes a member of the Freemasons.

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:22.940
Here's a rare photo.

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:25.021
Their ceremonies are highly secret.

00:07:25.402 --> 00:07:27.543
It shows Lindbergh in a Masonic lodge.

00:07:31.372 --> 00:07:32.493
The Freemasons of St.

00:07:32.553 --> 00:07:34.775
Louis make up a powerful business network,

00:07:35.175 --> 00:07:37.697
and these influential men become Lindbergh's sponsors.

00:07:38.898 --> 00:07:40.439
Bankers like Harold Bigsby,

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:43.361
investment experts like Harry Knight,

00:07:43.901 --> 00:07:45.663
or the owner of the local daily paper,

00:07:46.103 --> 00:07:46.904
Lansing Ray.

00:07:49.646 --> 00:07:51.867
Lindbergh raises $13,000,

00:07:52.188 --> 00:07:53.549
which he promises to pay back,

00:07:53.949 --> 00:07:56.371
and adds his own savings of $2,000.

00:07:59.459 --> 00:08:01.161
He takes the train to California,

00:08:02.362 --> 00:08:05.044
where he's finally found a constructor for his airplane.

00:08:06.706 --> 00:08:14.272
The Ryan Flying Company of San Diego proposes to build his plane within the budget and promises to complete the work in two months in their factory,

00:08:14.653 --> 00:08:17.275
a former canning plant that still smells of fish.

00:08:20.218 --> 00:08:24.201
Lindbergh and the engineer Donald Hall begin working on the plane's designs.

00:08:26.636 --> 00:08:29.757
Lindbergh insists on placing an enormous gas tank in front of him.

00:08:30.857 --> 00:08:32.878
A retractable periscope is created,

00:08:33.298 --> 00:08:34.978
allowing him a limited frontal view.

00:08:37.719 --> 00:08:41.300
Everything is calculated to maximize fuel storage capacity.

00:08:42.259 --> 00:08:44.321
There won't be a co-pilot or a navigator.

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:48.842
The structure is a sort of customized flying box built around Lindbergh.

00:08:53.302 --> 00:08:53.942
Early March.

00:08:54.302 --> 00:08:55.783
The steel fuselage is ready.

00:08:56.831 --> 00:08:57.932
It'll be covered with canvas,

00:08:58.273 --> 00:08:59.774
as will the wing's wooden frames.

00:09:02.655 --> 00:09:06.538
Lindbergh is a perfectionist and meticulously verifies each detail.

00:09:07.478 --> 00:09:10.760
At first the workers are blasé and skeptical about his project,

00:09:11.201 --> 00:09:14.103
but he wins them over with his perseverance and professionalism.

00:09:15.124 --> 00:09:16.564
They work overtime for Lindbergh,

00:09:16.625 --> 00:09:17.625
ten hours a day,

00:09:18.145 --> 00:09:20.327
and at night their wives sew together the canvas.

00:09:21.007 --> 00:09:22.528
This was the technique at the time.

00:09:23.589 --> 00:09:25.790
Only the nose cone is covered in metal.

00:09:26.692 --> 00:09:27.292
The engine,

00:09:27.532 --> 00:09:33.374
a nine-cylinder Wright J5C Whirlwind with 223 horsepower is mounted.

00:09:33.874 --> 00:09:34.634
It's a marvel,

00:09:35.034 --> 00:09:36.254
far ahead of its time.

00:09:37.335 --> 00:09:41.675
Lindbergh breaks it down and rebuilds it so many times that a mechanic finally asks,

00:09:41.976 --> 00:09:43.557
Why are you so obsessive?

00:09:44.216 --> 00:09:45.138
Lindbergh answers,

00:09:45.997 --> 00:09:47.217
Because I can't swim.

00:09:51.119 --> 00:09:52.960
Early April 1927.

00:09:53.539 --> 00:09:55.706
The plane receives its official call number,

00:09:56.588 --> 00:09:56.889
NX-211,

00:09:58.114 --> 00:09:59.598
and for all American planes,

00:09:59.838 --> 00:09:59.980
X-ray.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:00.801
For experimental,

00:10:01.421 --> 00:10:02.741
Ryan type NYP,

00:10:03.341 --> 00:10:03.841
New York,

00:10:03.923 --> 00:10:04.363
Paris.

00:10:08.586 --> 00:10:13.249
Lindbergh has the mechanics install a series of instruments which are state-of-the-art for the time,

00:10:13.668 --> 00:10:16.210
and which will enable him to fly in zero visibility,

00:10:19.513 --> 00:10:24.115
as well as a series of valves to control the fuel stored in his five tanks.

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:27.980
Facing the controls,

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:31.843
he installs the most uncomfortable wicker garden chair he can find,

00:10:32.343 --> 00:10:34.065
meant to keep him from falling asleep.

00:10:37.007 --> 00:10:37.307
April 27th,

00:10:37.347 --> 00:10:38.870
1927,

00:10:39.289 --> 00:10:41.432
Lindbergh's plane finally leaves the workshop.

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:48.383
Lindbergh demands every precaution be taken for this delicate maneuver preceding the final assembly.

00:10:49.803 --> 00:10:50.903
Lindbergh's in the car.

00:10:51.205 --> 00:10:51.725
He says,

00:10:52.684 --> 00:10:53.566
What finesse,

00:10:53.905 --> 00:10:54.706
what elegance,

00:10:54.986 --> 00:10:56.006
with its silver coat.

00:11:01.831 --> 00:11:03.751
The plane gets its large wings,

00:11:03.891 --> 00:11:04.731
27 feet,

00:11:04.932 --> 00:11:05.913
8 inches across.

00:11:06.312 --> 00:11:07.134
A new record.

00:11:07.514 --> 00:11:08.495
The plane is named

00:11:08.955 --> 00:11:09.835
Spirit of St.

00:11:09.936 --> 00:11:10.255
Louis.

00:11:15.983 --> 00:11:16.744
kept his bet,

00:11:17.225 --> 00:11:20.305
accompanied by his engineer and the rest of the Ryan team.

00:11:21.665 --> 00:11:23.906
60 days after having begun construction,

00:11:23.927 --> 00:11:25.487
the moments finally arrived.

00:11:26.087 --> 00:11:28.948
All of the calculations and planning have become reality.

00:11:35.831 --> 00:11:38.512
The miracle of aviation is reproduced,

00:11:39.153 --> 00:11:40.573
as it is with every takeoff.

00:11:42.334 --> 00:11:43.154
It flies,

00:11:43.954 --> 00:11:44.816
and quite well.

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:51.042
He arrives in New York on May 12th.

00:11:55.025 --> 00:11:57.667
The landing is difficult with no forward visibility.

00:12:03.852 --> 00:12:07.395
Lindbergh's surprised to discover that his arrival is very much awaited.

00:12:08.096 --> 00:12:11.378
The public and the press have rushed here to discover the face of their new hero.

00:12:14.361 --> 00:12:17.003
The crowd and all the commotion make him un-at-ease.

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:20.541
He's seen making an upset gesture.

00:12:22.162 --> 00:12:25.283
Then he's rapidly pulled away by his motor's manufacturer.

00:12:29.445 --> 00:12:31.186
Worried by what she reads in the press,

00:12:31.265 --> 00:12:32.687
his mother rushes to New York,

00:12:33.287 --> 00:12:34.447
which angers Lindbergh.

00:12:35.727 --> 00:12:37.668
They insist that mother and son kiss,

00:12:37.869 --> 00:12:39.350
but he categorically refuses.

00:12:39.610 --> 00:12:40.971
It's something they've never done.

00:12:43.587 --> 00:12:44.629
Reporter insists,

00:12:45.229 --> 00:12:45.669
but Mrs.

00:12:45.710 --> 00:12:46.210
Lindbergh,

00:12:46.309 --> 00:12:47.971
this may be the last time you see your son.

00:12:49.854 --> 00:12:51.174
Lindbergh hates the press.

00:12:53.336 --> 00:12:54.376
Do you have a girlfriend?

00:12:54.817 --> 00:12:54.937
No,

00:12:55.077 --> 00:12:55.639
he answers,

00:12:55.698 --> 00:12:56.658
becoming more awkward.

00:13:00.543 --> 00:13:03.125
The press nickname him the Lone Eagle.

00:13:05.706 --> 00:13:07.048
Yet women have a crush on him,

00:13:07.308 --> 00:13:09.870
with his movie actor looks and the dimple on his chin.

00:13:17.812 --> 00:13:18.312
At dawn,

00:13:18.832 --> 00:13:20.693
Lindbergh takes his plane out of its hangar.

00:13:21.673 --> 00:13:22.555
He hasn't slept.

00:13:22.774 --> 00:13:24.315
He's been waiting for the weather to change.

00:13:24.894 --> 00:13:26.816
They say it might clear up over the Atlantic.

00:13:27.375 --> 00:13:28.797
And he leaps at the opportunity.

00:13:31.018 --> 00:13:33.079
He fills up his tanks with oil and gas,

00:13:33.839 --> 00:13:35.399
verifying it's well filtered.

00:13:38.580 --> 00:13:40.821
His five gas tanks are filled to the maximum,

00:13:41.281 --> 00:13:42.902
and to compensate for all this weight,

00:13:43.262 --> 00:13:44.942
he eliminates superfluous equipment,

00:13:45.223 --> 00:13:45.863
like the battery,

00:13:46.083 --> 00:13:47.043
for his instrument light.

00:13:47.524 --> 00:13:47.964
Instead,

00:13:48.324 --> 00:13:49.845
he'll use a simple flashlight.

00:13:53.126 --> 00:13:54.666
He refuses to take a radio,

00:13:55.187 --> 00:13:56.067
or a parachute.

00:13:56.826 --> 00:13:57.427
They're too heavy.

00:13:58.875 --> 00:14:01.798
Only a small inflatable life raft bought on sale.

00:14:03.118 --> 00:14:04.259
And a thick flight suit,

00:14:04.599 --> 00:14:08.020
which he designed himself for protection from the cold at high altitudes.

00:14:19.385 --> 00:14:22.347
He takes only five sandwiches and a flask of water.

00:14:23.212 --> 00:14:23.792
Is that enough?

00:14:23.912 --> 00:14:24.712
Asks a reporter.

00:14:25.793 --> 00:14:27.053
With his usual dry humor,

00:14:27.113 --> 00:14:27.693
he answers,

00:14:28.834 --> 00:14:29.854
Enough to get to Paris.

00:14:30.454 --> 00:14:31.234
If I don't make it,

00:14:31.494 --> 00:14:32.155
I won't need them.

00:14:39.417 --> 00:14:40.937
The fateful moments arrived.

00:14:42.157 --> 00:14:43.878
He can't let down those who've helped him.

00:14:45.178 --> 00:14:47.459
But he knows the wind is blowing in the bad direction.

00:14:58.414 --> 00:15:00.000
He's nervous as his plane rolls.

00:15:00.100 --> 00:15:03.101
over the muddy ground covered with potholes from the constant rain.

00:15:04.861 --> 00:15:06.543
He did everything to lighten the plane,

00:15:06.562 --> 00:15:08.804
but it still weighs two and a half tons,

00:15:09.105 --> 00:15:10.644
more than double its weight empty.

00:15:11.806 --> 00:15:12.485
It's incredible.

00:15:13.826 --> 00:15:15.648
The fuselage shakes under the effort.

00:15:16.327 --> 00:15:17.568
The motor runs perfectly,

00:15:17.808 --> 00:15:19.370
but it lacks 30 more RPM,

00:15:19.389 --> 00:15:19.970
he says.

00:15:20.450 --> 00:15:21.831
He doesn't have full power.

00:15:24.087 --> 00:15:27.230
No plane's ever taken off with such a large load of fuel.

00:15:31.335 --> 00:15:34.638
The end of the runway is 300 meters away and he can't stop now.

00:15:34.938 --> 00:15:37.561
The telegraph lines are approaching dangerously fast.

00:15:42.426 --> 00:15:43.826
And he flies over them.

00:15:49.467 --> 00:15:50.067
It's Friday,

00:15:50.148 --> 00:15:50.307
May 20th,

00:15:50.308 --> 00:15:51.968
1927,

00:15:52.269 --> 00:15:54.149
at exactly 7.52 a.m.

00:15:58.071 --> 00:16:01.072
Airborne newsreel cameras follow him over Long Island.

00:16:04.413 --> 00:16:05.994
He flies further away.

00:16:07.053 --> 00:16:09.595
No camera filmed his flight beyond this point.

00:16:14.764 --> 00:16:16.625
I find myself in a blanket of fog,

00:16:16.706 --> 00:16:17.405
Lindbergh says.

00:16:18.026 --> 00:16:19.988
A thick curtain that's moving with me.

00:16:20.829 --> 00:16:22.091
What's beyond the curtain?

00:16:25.573 --> 00:16:26.534
Using the telescope,

00:16:26.774 --> 00:16:27.576
I look in front of me.

00:16:34.140 --> 00:16:35.221
I come out of the fog.

00:16:36.262 --> 00:16:37.583
The horizon brightens.

00:16:42.871 --> 00:16:44.634
The wings shake in the turbulence.

00:16:45.254 --> 00:16:45.935
Will they break?

00:16:52.922 --> 00:16:55.504
It's the first time that Lindbergh's ever adventured over water.

00:16:58.748 --> 00:17:01.831
A long flight over the ocean before reaching Nova Scotia.

00:17:06.627 --> 00:17:08.850
It's my first test of navigation over the ocean,

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:09.950
Lindbergh says.

00:17:10.831 --> 00:17:12.050
As I didn't sleep last night,

00:17:12.131 --> 00:17:13.711
time seems to be passing slowly.

00:17:15.933 --> 00:17:18.735
My cruising speed isn't going over 100 miles per hour.

00:17:21.037 --> 00:17:23.958
The small bubble in the center indicates that the plane is stable.

00:17:25.419 --> 00:17:27.200
But the altimeter reading is dangerous.

00:17:27.740 --> 00:17:28.520
It's nearing zero.

00:17:28.941 --> 00:17:29.562
I'm too low.

00:17:30.741 --> 00:17:32.502
I just miss hitting a sailboat's mast.

00:17:37.019 --> 00:17:39.461
I'm so heavy that I'm having a lot of trouble climbing.

00:17:42.443 --> 00:17:43.384
I have to climb,

00:17:44.086 --> 00:17:44.746
but gradually,

00:17:45.307 --> 00:17:46.367
to be careful of the motor.

00:17:57.195 --> 00:17:59.739
He's nervous about the plane's light frame and canvas.

00:18:01.079 --> 00:18:03.521
They were never meant to stand up to such heavy turbulence.

00:18:12.188 --> 00:18:12.728
Miraculously,

00:18:13.109 --> 00:18:14.370
the storm dies down.

00:18:17.572 --> 00:18:18.232
At nightfall,

00:18:18.613 --> 00:18:19.974
Lindbergh reaches Nova Scotia.

00:18:23.696 --> 00:18:24.097
And then,

00:18:24.457 --> 00:18:24.917
finally,

00:18:25.397 --> 00:18:26.999
he heads out over the Atlantic.

00:18:31.582 --> 00:18:33.443
He disappears into the night.

00:18:41.872 --> 00:18:43.513
At the 18th hour of the flight,

00:18:43.553 --> 00:18:45.214
he's caught in a magnetic storm.

00:18:45.794 --> 00:18:47.755
His compass is completely disoriented.

00:18:48.275 --> 00:18:49.055
The spirit of St.

00:18:49.095 --> 00:18:50.136
Louis is in the clouds,

00:18:50.216 --> 00:18:52.057
flying in circles without a landmark.

00:18:52.677 --> 00:18:53.437
Without a hope.

00:18:56.419 --> 00:18:58.099
It's one of the flight's worst moments.

00:19:00.759 --> 00:19:02.081
With his flashlight in his mouth,

00:19:02.362 --> 00:19:04.723
he tries getting back on course with his instruments.

00:19:05.223 --> 00:19:05.865
But finally,

00:19:06.265 --> 00:19:09.628
it's a clearing in the clouds which allows him to navigate by the stars.

00:19:10.870 --> 00:19:12.851
He can once again set course for Europe.

00:19:14.033 --> 00:19:14.833
He relaxes,

00:19:15.173 --> 00:19:16.055
a fatal error.

00:19:18.497 --> 00:19:20.919
He doesn't notice he's descending towards the ocean.

00:19:23.172 --> 00:19:25.353
He hasn't slept for over 40 hours,

00:19:25.553 --> 00:19:26.972
and he can't take it any longer.

00:19:27.692 --> 00:19:28.413
It's torture,

00:19:28.453 --> 00:19:28.994
he says,

00:19:29.334 --> 00:19:30.974
dazed by the motor's loud noise.

00:19:33.755 --> 00:19:36.416
The human body was never meant to endure such torture.

00:19:37.076 --> 00:19:37.996
My back's stiff,

00:19:38.135 --> 00:19:39.096
my eyes are burning,

00:19:39.477 --> 00:19:42.577
and what I want most in the world is to lay down and sleep.

00:19:45.379 --> 00:19:49.980
Why is the need to sleep stronger and more forceful over the sea than over land?

00:19:51.759 --> 00:19:53.483
Is it because there's nothing to look at?

00:19:53.964 --> 00:19:54.928
Nothing but waves?

00:19:57.875 --> 00:19:59.778
He loses consciousness for a few moments.

00:20:00.420 --> 00:20:01.641
He nearly crashes.

00:20:02.741 --> 00:20:04.663
He pulls up just above the waves.

00:20:06.786 --> 00:20:07.987
At the 27th hour,

00:20:08.247 --> 00:20:09.988
he thinks he sees a spot on the ocean.

00:20:12.911 --> 00:20:14.951
He leans over and sees a fishing boat.

00:20:15.633 --> 00:20:16.192
He yells,

00:20:16.433 --> 00:20:17.294
Where's Ireland?

00:20:18.255 --> 00:20:20.676
As if the fishermen could hear him over the motor's roar.

00:20:21.738 --> 00:20:22.759
Even without an answer,

00:20:22.939 --> 00:20:24.559
he believes his direction is correct,

00:20:24.760 --> 00:20:25.320
and it is.

00:20:26.321 --> 00:20:27.702
Ireland soon appears.

00:20:31.900 --> 00:20:37.722
Lindbergh's now become the first man ever to link North America to Europe on a solo voyage.

00:20:41.526 --> 00:20:42.826
If Lindbergh stopped here,

00:20:43.186 --> 00:20:44.386
he'd already be famous.

00:20:45.168 --> 00:20:47.328
But he's competing for the Orteg Prize.

00:20:47.608 --> 00:20:49.190
He must go all the way to Paris.

00:20:51.912 --> 00:20:54.393
He follows Cornwall's rough and rocky coast,

00:20:56.193 --> 00:20:58.576
and crosses the English Channel like in a dream.

00:21:01.523 --> 00:21:02.865
His fatigue's disappeared.

00:21:03.505 --> 00:21:04.326
He's alert now,

00:21:04.445 --> 00:21:09.328
flying high on optimism as he passes over Deauville at the end of his second day.

00:21:11.230 --> 00:21:12.770
He plays at castle hopping,

00:21:13.330 --> 00:21:16.373
and he finally allows himself to eat his first sandwich.

00:21:17.972 --> 00:21:20.654
He's about to throw his paper bag and crumbs out the window,

00:21:20.775 --> 00:21:21.915
but changes his mind.

00:21:22.915 --> 00:21:24.836
These fields are so clean and pretty,

00:21:24.916 --> 00:21:25.478
he thinks.

00:21:25.958 --> 00:21:28.880
I wouldn't want that to be my first contact with the French soil.

00:21:30.859 --> 00:21:31.901
The news spreads.

00:21:32.240 --> 00:21:33.521
Lindbergh's been spotted.

00:21:35.242 --> 00:21:36.503
It's 10.20 p.m.

00:21:36.644 --> 00:21:37.543
on this Saturday,

00:21:37.723 --> 00:21:37.964
May 21st,

00:21:37.984 --> 00:21:39.885
1927.

00:21:42.027 --> 00:21:45.108
After 33 hours and 30 minutes of flying time,

00:21:45.449 --> 00:21:47.431
Lindbergh arrives in Paris.

00:21:48.971 --> 00:21:51.173
The cameramen aren't allowed to approach the plane,

00:21:51.492 --> 00:21:52.554
much less Lindbergh,

00:21:52.773 --> 00:21:55.495
who's swiftly taken to the American ambassador's residence.

00:22:00.076 --> 00:22:00.876
The next morning,

00:22:01.437 --> 00:22:04.559
Sunday's first curiosity seekers come to see Lindbergh.

00:22:05.219 --> 00:22:09.801
They applaud outside the residence as he appears in a suit lent by the ambassador's valet.

00:22:11.461 --> 00:22:12.563
He's finally slept.

00:22:13.463 --> 00:22:15.525
His first gesture is to shout his joy,

00:22:16.005 --> 00:22:18.346
using the only three words he knows in French.

00:22:18.907 --> 00:22:20.067
Vive la France.

00:22:22.128 --> 00:22:26.211
He's then informed that a telephone connection's been established with his mother in the U.S.

00:22:26.831 --> 00:22:27.371
He says,

00:22:27.751 --> 00:22:28.592
I've had a good trip.

00:22:29.439 --> 00:22:30.140
She responds,

00:22:30.500 --> 00:22:31.341
get some rest.

00:22:33.303 --> 00:22:34.024
Myron Herrick,

00:22:34.204 --> 00:22:35.265
the American ambassador,

00:22:35.346 --> 00:22:36.246
tells the crowd,

00:22:37.507 --> 00:22:39.389
this young man coming from the West,

00:22:39.909 --> 00:22:41.651
he brings America's soul to you.

00:22:42.292 --> 00:22:44.134
America's heart beats for France.

00:22:44.714 --> 00:22:45.695
When he returns home,

00:22:46.076 --> 00:22:49.740
he can say that France truly loves the people of the United States.

00:22:55.523 --> 00:22:59.987
Paris is home to the largest crowds ever since the signing of the armistice on November 11th,

00:22:59.989 --> 00:23:01.489
1918.

00:23:20.544 --> 00:23:20.664
The

00:23:20.704 --> 00:23:23.206
Americans are asking for Lindbergh to come home.

00:23:24.003 --> 00:23:26.166
He goes to Bourget to prepare his departure.

00:23:26.666 --> 00:23:28.128
He would have liked to see more of Europe,

00:23:28.589 --> 00:23:32.073
but he only has time for short trips to Brussels and London.

00:23:35.397 --> 00:23:38.402
Lindbergh is impressed by his invitation from a true monarch,

00:23:38.741 --> 00:23:39.602
the Belgian king.

00:23:40.884 --> 00:23:42.105
He admires Brussels'

00:23:42.145 --> 00:23:42.445
beauty.

00:23:44.920 --> 00:23:46.161
The mayor proclaims,

00:23:46.762 --> 00:23:48.865
your victory is that of all humanity.

00:23:49.686 --> 00:23:52.369
You've done more than anyone to bring mankind together.

00:23:59.317 --> 00:24:00.017
The next day,

00:24:00.538 --> 00:24:02.981
wearing a leather jacket offered by French aviators,

00:24:03.301 --> 00:24:04.483
he takes off for London.

00:24:09.419 --> 00:24:13.821
He's welcomed by a crowd of one million people massed at the Croydon Airfield.

00:24:14.962 --> 00:24:16.542
He's unaware that all around the globe,

00:24:16.543 --> 00:24:18.003
in cities like Berlin and Tokyo,

00:24:18.523 --> 00:24:21.024
huge crowds are joyously celebrating his victory.

00:24:22.005 --> 00:24:23.405
To save himself from the crowd,

00:24:23.645 --> 00:24:25.726
he's obliged to climb up the control tower.

00:24:30.443 --> 00:24:31.084
He yells,

00:24:32.164 --> 00:24:33.986
this is even worse than at Bourget.

00:24:35.047 --> 00:24:35.807
I mean to say,

00:24:36.747 --> 00:24:37.468
it's even better.

00:24:42.309 --> 00:24:45.432
He's beginning to understand the true signification of his exploit,

00:24:46.031 --> 00:24:47.913
which has made him a prisoner to fame.

00:24:52.415 --> 00:24:52.935
In London,

00:24:53.276 --> 00:24:55.136
he visits the home of King George V.

00:24:55.978 --> 00:24:57.379
A young woman hurries toward him.

00:24:57.939 --> 00:24:59.980
Lindbergh's autograph is already worth a small...

00:25:00.099 --> 00:25:00.519
fortune.

00:25:07.001 --> 00:25:09.363
George V dares to ask the simple question,

00:25:09.962 --> 00:25:12.943
during the flight how did you satisfy the needs of nature?

00:25:15.124 --> 00:25:16.045
Lindbergh responds,

00:25:16.724 --> 00:25:18.986
I used a thermos that I threw out into the ocean.

00:25:19.865 --> 00:25:21.965
Yet this doesn't seem to be the true case.

00:25:22.586 --> 00:25:24.467
His famous flight suit had to be thrown out.

00:25:24.807 --> 00:25:26.548
It was too soiled to be reused.

00:25:31.251 --> 00:25:33.254
And still wearing his new leather jacket,

00:25:33.255 --> 00:25:34.975
he boards the ocean liner Memphis,

00:25:35.375 --> 00:25:37.017
which will take him home to the U.S.

00:25:38.538 --> 00:25:41.120
He's of course accompanied by his precious Spirit of St.

00:25:41.180 --> 00:25:41.460
Louis,

00:25:41.760 --> 00:25:43.362
dismantled and stored in crates.

00:25:44.263 --> 00:25:44.783
On board,

00:25:45.083 --> 00:25:50.248
the radio plays the first of the more than 200 songs that will be written about Lindbergh and his historic feat.

00:26:30.923 --> 00:26:37.187
21 cannon shots to welcome Lindy home.

00:26:37.248 --> 00:26:39.329
A welcoming Lindbergh is reserved,

00:26:39.330 --> 00:26:39.889
of course,

00:26:40.269 --> 00:26:40.931
for his mother.

00:26:43.111 --> 00:26:44.933
Angeline Land Lindbergh says,

00:26:47.515 --> 00:26:49.456
I'm very impressed by his simplicity,

00:26:49.777 --> 00:26:50.377
his dignity,

00:26:50.917 --> 00:26:52.519
the depth of my son's soul.

00:26:57.015 --> 00:26:58.656
The president of the United States,

00:26:58.817 --> 00:26:59.736
Calvin Coolidge,

00:27:00.256 --> 00:27:01.137
makes him a colonel,

00:27:01.717 --> 00:27:04.198
a rank rarely achieved at his young age.

00:27:10.160 --> 00:27:11.741
Three days later in New York,

00:27:12.041 --> 00:27:15.463
the United States witnesses the greatest ticker tape parade in its history.

00:27:18.365 --> 00:27:23.526
He's only now becoming fully conscious of how his life's been completely transformed.

00:27:26.263 --> 00:27:31.749
again be able to walk alone in the street or enter a store without causing a riot.

00:27:34.211 --> 00:27:37.714
His loved ones will never be completely safe from strangers.

00:27:39.880 --> 00:27:40.441
As of now,

00:27:40.901 --> 00:27:43.804
people try tearing off pieces of his clothing for souvenirs.

00:27:44.404 --> 00:27:46.567
He's followed everywhere like the celebrities of the time.

00:27:47.047 --> 00:27:49.128
Hollywood's movie producers send him telegrams.

00:27:49.950 --> 00:27:53.272
Paramount and Universal offer him contracts for $700,000,

00:27:53.373 --> 00:27:54.094
but he refuses.

00:27:54.534 --> 00:27:56.135
Acting is not his career.

00:27:56.997 --> 00:27:57.396
But money,

00:27:57.537 --> 00:27:58.198
nonetheless,

00:27:58.397 --> 00:27:59.419
begins pouring in.

00:28:00.963 --> 00:28:01.443
To start,

00:28:01.825 --> 00:28:03.205
the Frenchman Raymond Orteg,

00:28:03.465 --> 00:28:05.447
who was at the origin of this entire adventure,

00:28:05.728 --> 00:28:06.989
awards him his prize money,

00:28:07.348 --> 00:28:09.570
the famous check for $25,000,

00:28:10.271 --> 00:28:12.134
quite a considerable sum at the time.

00:28:16.156 --> 00:28:17.498
And that's just the beginning.

00:28:18.418 --> 00:28:20.200
The billionaire Henry Guggenheim,

00:28:20.600 --> 00:28:22.382
one of aviation's great sponsors,

00:28:22.742 --> 00:28:25.365
offers $50,000 to send the spirit of St.

00:28:25.404 --> 00:28:27.886
Louis on tour to the 48 states of the Union.

00:28:33.376 --> 00:28:34.637
In a four-month period,

00:28:35.038 --> 00:28:38.120
Lindbergh succeeds in greatly promoting air transportation.

00:28:40.423 --> 00:28:42.364
The number of airfields is about to double.

00:28:43.404 --> 00:28:45.307
Many of them will carry the hero's name.

00:28:46.047 --> 00:28:46.988
Countless children,

00:28:47.107 --> 00:28:49.529
towns and parks will also be named after him.

00:28:50.550 --> 00:28:52.413
An Oklahoma Indian tribe names him

00:28:52.812 --> 00:28:55.355
Chitokaka, the greatest of the White Eagles.

00:28:58.551 --> 00:29:00.574
He continues his tour with the spirit of St.

00:29:00.634 --> 00:29:00.933
Louis,

00:29:01.375 --> 00:29:01.855
direction

00:29:02.235 --> 00:29:02.955
Latin America.

00:29:06.418 --> 00:29:07.019
In Mexico,

00:29:07.299 --> 00:29:09.361
at the end of December 1927,

00:29:09.722 --> 00:29:11.463
he's given a very particular mission.

00:29:12.884 --> 00:29:18.730
His great popularity is needed to help Ambassador Dwight Moreau to improve Mexican-American relations.

00:29:32.316 --> 00:29:34.898
The signs of a warming in relations come rapidly,

00:29:35.499 --> 00:29:40.722
demonstrated by this exceptional welcome Lindbergh written in sand in Mexico's great arena.

00:29:47.269 --> 00:29:49.691
After receiving the poor bull's ears and tail,

00:29:50.531 --> 00:29:54.835
Lindbergh spends Christmas in a real family setting with the ambassador's family.

00:30:02.779 --> 00:30:04.540
All the demands of his public life,

00:30:05.000 --> 00:30:07.322
he rarely has occasions for meeting young women.

00:30:09.183 --> 00:30:11.684
He's interested in the ambassador's second daughter,

00:30:12.065 --> 00:30:12.605
Anne Moreau,

00:30:13.006 --> 00:30:14.105
21 years old.

00:30:15.086 --> 00:30:16.167
She's a literary girl,

00:30:16.407 --> 00:30:16.927
a writer,

00:30:17.347 --> 00:30:19.328
whose poems are sensitive and sensual.

00:30:20.389 --> 00:30:20.990
She's timid,

00:30:21.490 --> 00:30:24.831
and not so much in love as she is subjugated by Lindbergh,

00:30:25.292 --> 00:30:27.993
this man who has a very particular conception of things.

00:30:30.875 --> 00:30:31.135
Later...

00:30:31.315 --> 00:30:31.915
he'd write,

00:30:32.516 --> 00:30:34.597
A girl should come from a healthy family.

00:30:35.217 --> 00:30:39.920
My experience in breeding animals on our farm had taught me the importance of good heredity.

00:30:40.820 --> 00:30:44.322
The physical qualities I wanted in a woman were good health,

00:30:44.842 --> 00:30:45.443
good sight,

00:30:45.963 --> 00:30:46.483
and hearing.

00:30:47.584 --> 00:30:48.625
But most importantly,

00:30:49.125 --> 00:30:51.266
I wanted to marry a girl who liked flying.

00:30:54.288 --> 00:30:55.608
For two such timid people,

00:30:56.029 --> 00:30:59.510
the motor's rumble will help them get around having to engage in love talk.

00:31:01.139 --> 00:31:05.163
She's more seduced by the hero than in love with him and agrees to marry him on May 27th,

00:31:05.163 --> 00:31:06.884
1929.

00:31:08.845 --> 00:31:10.546
And one need only see them together,

00:31:11.206 --> 00:31:14.449
how he directs this frightened bird with his hand and with his eyes.

00:31:19.531 --> 00:31:23.714
The press follows them everywhere and calls them the couple of the skies.

00:31:29.419 --> 00:31:32.040
He teaches her piloting and aerial navigation.

00:31:35.182 --> 00:31:38.423
He promises her beautiful voyages and great adventures.

00:31:41.845 --> 00:31:43.965
Yet Lindbergh doesn't seem truly happy,

00:31:44.305 --> 00:31:46.186
and it shows in this rare film,

00:31:46.726 --> 00:31:48.907
shot at a small Californian airfield.

00:31:52.129 --> 00:31:57.151
Anne Moreau prepares to become the first woman in the United States to earn a glider's pilot license.

00:32:07.135 --> 00:32:08.436
On this particular afternoon,

00:32:08.937 --> 00:32:11.637
her friends try warming up the atmosphere around Anne,

00:32:12.137 --> 00:32:12.798
whose smile,

00:32:12.918 --> 00:32:13.498
as usual,

00:32:13.758 --> 00:32:14.758
shows some sadness.

00:32:18.298 --> 00:32:21.220
...is seated off to the side with a very serious demeanor.

00:32:22.080 --> 00:32:25.480
Is it because the cameras have once again intruded into his private life,

00:32:25.941 --> 00:32:28.182
or because he's just learned that Anne is pregnant?

00:32:29.122 --> 00:32:30.982
It's good news in and of itself,

00:32:31.363 --> 00:32:34.624
but it might create obstacles for his projects of upcoming voyages.

00:32:41.539 --> 00:32:43.061
He craves more exploits,

00:32:43.281 --> 00:32:44.022
the unknown.

00:32:44.442 --> 00:32:45.804
He needs to depart again.

00:32:48.227 --> 00:32:51.549
He hires Lockheed to construct an ultra-modern airplane,

00:32:51.990 --> 00:32:52.730
the Sirius.

00:32:54.272 --> 00:32:56.654
And he embarks the pregnant Anne Moreau aboard,

00:32:57.056 --> 00:32:59.258
where she's confined within this tight cockpit,

00:32:59.678 --> 00:33:00.659
devoid of comfort.

00:33:16.344 --> 00:33:20.829
They'll break the North American cross-continental speed record by three hours.

00:33:24.853 --> 00:33:25.634
At their arrival,

00:33:26.115 --> 00:33:30.340
everyone except Lindbergh can see that she's endured a torture within this steel box.

00:33:31.581 --> 00:33:33.663
Her pained smile shows her fatigue.

00:33:45.764 --> 00:33:47.125
One year after their marriage,

00:33:47.546 --> 00:33:47.865
June 22nd,

00:33:47.885 --> 00:33:49.307
1930,

00:33:50.008 --> 00:33:51.389
Charles Lindbergh Jr.

00:33:51.429 --> 00:33:52.009
is born.

00:33:53.009 --> 00:33:56.733
America celebrates his birth as if the boy were a royal descendant.

00:33:58.575 --> 00:34:01.497
Lindbergh refuses to show his son to the story-hungry press.

00:34:02.158 --> 00:34:02.817
But even on his

00:34:03.879 --> 00:34:05.180
420-acre estate near New York,

00:34:05.661 --> 00:34:08.603
he can't keep totally hidden from the camera's telephoto lenses.

00:34:16.735 --> 00:34:19.476
In the social context of America's Great Depression,

00:34:20.056 --> 00:34:24.257
a vengeful press begins attacking Lindbergh for being paid to do nothing.

00:34:28.938 --> 00:34:33.280
He has the title of technical director for the first trans-American air route,

00:34:33.780 --> 00:34:35.860
which has been named the Lindbergh Line.

00:34:39.362 --> 00:34:41.322
For the airline's advertising films,

00:34:41.822 --> 00:34:44.364
he flies alongside the first commercial flights.

00:34:45.835 --> 00:34:47.618
He's more and more present in the media,

00:34:48.339 --> 00:34:51.745
and he's receiving an increasing number of threats against his child.

00:34:55.732 --> 00:34:56.433
March 1st,

00:34:56.434 --> 00:34:58.557
1932.

00:34:58.558 --> 00:34:59.138
Admiral Lindbergh,

00:34:59.298 --> 00:35:00.000
who is once again...

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:03.121
One pregnant says goodnight to her boy at 8 o'clock.

00:35:06.445 --> 00:35:07.826
He quietly falls asleep.

00:35:08.827 --> 00:35:10.927
His nanny is sleeping in the adjoining room.

00:35:16.893 --> 00:35:19.934
She returns to her boy's room at about 10 p.m.

00:35:20.695 --> 00:35:21.815
His crib is empty.

00:35:22.836 --> 00:35:25.599
A ladder outside is the sign of a kidnapping.

00:35:27.884 --> 00:35:31.266
A badly scribbled ransom note demands $50,000.

00:35:36.548 --> 00:35:41.032
The kidnapping of the Lindbergh baby becomes the human interest headline of the century.

00:35:41.811 --> 00:35:43.713
Daily papers see their sales increase.

00:35:44.193 --> 00:35:46.074
Lindbergh is definitely good for business.

00:35:48.836 --> 00:35:50.617
An employee of the house is suspected.

00:35:51.217 --> 00:35:54.639
She commits suicide rather than having to confess her affair with the head butler.

00:35:56.947 --> 00:35:58.708
Lindbergh seems oddly calm,

00:35:59.548 --> 00:36:01.090
but he tries everything in his power.

00:36:02.831 --> 00:36:06.393
This Puritan even goes so far as to solicit the help of gangsters.

00:36:07.452 --> 00:36:11.134
Al Capone offers a $10,000 reward for any helpful information.

00:36:13.076 --> 00:36:19.639
Lindbergh authorizes that these private home movies be shown to help people identify the baby with his famous dimpled chin.

00:36:23.172 --> 00:36:24.072
To save his boy,

00:36:24.373 --> 00:36:24.913
on April 2nd,

00:36:24.914 --> 00:36:26.313
1932,

00:36:27.175 --> 00:36:33.358
Lindbergh has someone hand over the ransom money in this suburban New York cemetery while closely surveying the operation.

00:36:36.880 --> 00:36:38.662
But the Lindberghs receive no news.

00:36:38.942 --> 00:36:40.543
The search is intensified.

00:36:50.835 --> 00:36:51.576
One month later,

00:36:51.976 --> 00:36:53.877
the poor baby's dead body is found,

00:36:54.117 --> 00:36:56.138
partially eaten by wild animals.

00:37:01.440 --> 00:37:03.822
It's established that he died during the kidnapping.

00:37:07.603 --> 00:37:09.984
The kidnapper dropped him from up on the ladder.

00:37:13.706 --> 00:37:15.367
Anne Moreau cries constantly.

00:37:20.915 --> 00:37:22.896
Lindbergh doesn't want their son buried.

00:37:23.616 --> 00:37:26.297
He wants to avoid the tomb becoming a tourist attraction.

00:37:29.259 --> 00:37:30.899
So he takes off for the Atlantic,

00:37:31.239 --> 00:37:36.000
at practically the same spot where five years earlier he began his voyage to glory.

00:37:37.501 --> 00:37:39.562
He throws his son's ashes into the ocean,

00:37:39.922 --> 00:37:40.462
and with them,

00:37:41.022 --> 00:37:42.423
a small part of himself.

00:37:48.983 --> 00:37:50.965
The hero will never be the same.

00:37:56.126 --> 00:37:59.349
A man's been arrested and accused of his son's kidnapping.

00:38:00.789 --> 00:38:03.851
His trial will mark a decisive test in Lindbergh's life.

00:38:04.652 --> 00:38:07.253
The papers call it the trial of the century.

00:38:09.333 --> 00:38:09.934
For a year,

00:38:10.233 --> 00:38:12.315
Lindbergh attends every day of the trial.

00:38:14.824 --> 00:38:17.244
The accused is named Bruno Hauptmann,

00:38:17.824 --> 00:38:19.786
a German immigrant and a carpenter.

00:38:23.367 --> 00:38:25.686
The police find the ransom money at his home,

00:38:25.927 --> 00:38:28.028
clumsily hidden in oil containers.

00:38:30.108 --> 00:38:31.228
But did he work alone?

00:38:31.849 --> 00:38:32.909
And is he even guilty?

00:38:34.829 --> 00:38:36.291
He consistently denies it,

00:38:37.311 --> 00:38:39.672
throughout the prosecutor's relentless questioning.

00:38:46.902 --> 00:38:49.604
Hauptmann's attorney advises that he keep his courage.

00:38:51.045 --> 00:38:54.128
The jury finds him guilty and he's put to death in the electric chair,

00:38:54.327 --> 00:38:57.670
even though today doubts still remain over his guilt.

00:38:59.452 --> 00:39:00.412
The trial is finished,

00:39:00.793 --> 00:39:02.014
the guilty party is punished,

00:39:02.293 --> 00:39:03.454
accomplices ignored.

00:39:07.203 --> 00:39:08.584
The reporter's harassment,

00:39:08.985 --> 00:39:11.667
the seller of scale models of the famous wooden ladder,

00:39:11.987 --> 00:39:13.688
and even fake hair from his boy,

00:39:14.209 --> 00:39:15.870
all of this destroys Lindbergh,

00:39:16.571 --> 00:39:17.151
who says,

00:39:18.312 --> 00:39:20.354
the American people are a primitive people.

00:39:20.994 --> 00:39:22.276
They're morally inferior,

00:39:22.676 --> 00:39:25.838
which is proved by their morbid curiosity for this case.

00:39:30.443 --> 00:39:36.327
An irresponsible press that sets no limit for itself is one of democracy's great dangers.

00:39:37.788 --> 00:39:38.588
He concludes,

00:39:39.568 --> 00:39:42.010
democracy itself can be dangerous.

00:39:47.112 --> 00:39:48.853
The Lindberghs flee the U.S.

00:39:49.253 --> 00:39:50.394
and to remain anonymous,

00:39:50.954 --> 00:39:52.576
they sail on this small ship.

00:39:54.996 --> 00:39:56.398
But on their arrival in England,

00:39:56.438 --> 00:39:59.880
the cameramen are still there and it's impossible to hide their

00:40:00.039 --> 00:40:00.699
Second son,

00:40:01.121 --> 00:40:01.440
John.

00:40:11.045 --> 00:40:12.646
The anxiety returns.

00:40:14.987 --> 00:40:15.168
Yet,

00:40:15.387 --> 00:40:18.889
it gradually calms down as they reach their new home in Kent.

00:40:19.829 --> 00:40:22.291
Charles rediscovers a peaceful family setting.

00:40:26.663 --> 00:40:30.565
It's at this time that Lindbergh will become seduced by Nazi Germany.

00:40:35.027 --> 00:40:36.186
Field Marshal Goering,

00:40:36.626 --> 00:40:39.949
second in power to Hitler and commander of the German Air Force,

00:40:40.328 --> 00:40:43.969
is encouraged by the American government to invite the Lindberghs for a visit.

00:40:45.089 --> 00:40:48.030
It's hoped that Lindbergh can ascertain Germany's military strength.

00:40:55.427 --> 00:40:55.969
Messerschmitt,

00:40:56.268 --> 00:40:57.790
the famous aircraft constructor,

00:40:58.251 --> 00:41:01.556
warmly receives them and invites Charles to fly his latest plane.

00:41:05.481 --> 00:41:06.723
Accompanied by a U.S.

00:41:06.804 --> 00:41:07.704
military attache,

00:41:08.545 --> 00:41:11.690
he's visibly impressed during his visit to the Messerschmitt factories.

00:41:12.844 --> 00:41:15.686
But what he sees isn't exactly the real situation.

00:41:16.507 --> 00:41:18.548
Hitler's Germany has a modern air force,

00:41:19.068 --> 00:41:20.591
but it's not yet powerful enough.

00:41:21.391 --> 00:41:22.112
In his report,

00:41:22.192 --> 00:41:25.515
Lindbergh presents an illusion which is costly for Western democracies,

00:41:25.815 --> 00:41:28.237
who are persuaded that Hitler's become invincible.

00:41:31.640 --> 00:41:34.222
Lindbergh becomes enthralled by the Nazi regime.

00:41:34.963 --> 00:41:35.523
He writes,

00:41:37.355 --> 00:41:41.178
There reigns a spirit that I've not seen in any other country.

00:41:42.259 --> 00:41:44.201
Everything is order and efficiency,

00:41:44.922 --> 00:41:47.943
in contrast to the disorder that reigns in France.

00:41:52.226 --> 00:41:53.507
Lindbergh also states,

00:41:54.307 --> 00:41:56.149
we should not be mistaken about our enemy.

00:41:56.929 --> 00:41:59.132
The real enemy is the Soviet Union.

00:41:59.831 --> 00:42:02.673
He considers Hitler to be less a threat than Stalin.

00:42:06.407 --> 00:42:12.012
It's understandable why Field Marshal Goering presented him with the service cross of the German Eagle.

00:42:14.253 --> 00:42:18.155
Lindbergh takes on a new mission of defending the superiority of the white race.

00:42:23.079 --> 00:42:24.800
On his family's return to the U.S.,

00:42:24.860 --> 00:42:26.302
he preaches his philosophy.

00:42:27.663 --> 00:42:29.603
America is divided over his message.

00:42:30.045 --> 00:42:30.445
For some,

00:42:30.525 --> 00:42:31.286
he's still a hero.

00:42:31.766 --> 00:42:32.306
For others,

00:42:32.586 --> 00:42:33.626
he's Hitler's messenger.

00:42:37.271 --> 00:42:38.693
President Roosevelt receives him.

00:42:39.313 --> 00:42:42.215
Officially it's to thank him for his report on Germany's air power.

00:42:42.755 --> 00:42:44.596
But it's also to try to win him over.

00:42:45.717 --> 00:42:46.496
It doesn't work.

00:42:49.518 --> 00:42:52.099
Nazi Germany launches its conquest of Europe.

00:42:54.181 --> 00:42:58.023
European democracies ask for help from the US and from President Roosevelt.

00:43:00.105 --> 00:43:05.728
People of Europe who are defending themselves do not ask us to do their fighting.

00:43:06.675 --> 00:43:09.818
They ask us for the implements of war,

00:43:10.418 --> 00:43:11.278
the planes,

00:43:11.340 --> 00:43:12.079
the tanks,

00:43:12.159 --> 00:43:12.740
the guns.

00:43:12.760 --> 00:43:17.923
Summer 1940.

00:43:18.505 --> 00:43:19.806
France is defeated.

00:43:30.547 --> 00:43:34.971
Lindbergh uses his notoriety to maintain the public opinion's opposition to Roosevelt,

00:43:35.250 --> 00:43:36.751
who wants to enter into the war.

00:43:39.632 --> 00:43:42.894
Lindbergh becomes the leader of a vast isolationist movement,

00:43:43.275 --> 00:43:44.235
America First.

00:43:49.219 --> 00:43:53.342
Lindbergh may even have been able to win election for President of the United States,

00:43:54.241 --> 00:43:55.682
at great cost to Europe.

00:44:05.035 --> 00:44:09.937
When we in this country have a right to think of the welfare of America first,

00:44:13.859 --> 00:44:19.761
no one can make us fight abroad unless we ourselves are willing to do so.

00:44:19.762 --> 00:44:28.465
1941.

00:44:28.826 --> 00:44:31.366
The Germans are advancing victoriously in Russia.

00:44:36.395 --> 00:44:39.259
During a famous radio address broadcast September 11th,

00:44:39.358 --> 00:44:40.659
1941,

00:44:41.160 --> 00:44:45.925
Lindbergh lays blame on what he calls war agitators working within the United States,

00:44:46.405 --> 00:44:48.268
trying to force the country into the war.

00:44:48.868 --> 00:44:49.608
They're the British,

00:44:50.109 --> 00:44:51.350
the Roosevelt administration,

00:44:51.570 --> 00:44:52.291
and the Jews.

00:44:52.992 --> 00:44:57.297
Lindbergh predicts they will be among the first to feel the war's consequences.

00:44:59.178 --> 00:44:59.639
Anne Moreau

00:45:00.067 --> 00:45:01.468
Who proofreads his speeches,

00:45:01.768 --> 00:45:02.668
thinks as he does.

00:45:03.329 --> 00:45:03.529
Yet,

00:45:03.849 --> 00:45:04.910
she fears a scandal,

00:45:05.170 --> 00:45:06.010
and with good reason,

00:45:06.390 --> 00:45:07.551
because it arrives quickly.

00:45:08.632 --> 00:45:11.333
The press denounces his anti-Semitism and labels him,

00:45:11.813 --> 00:45:13.474
America's most dangerous man.

00:45:22.628 --> 00:45:23.569
Three months later,

00:45:24.169 --> 00:45:25.570
Pearl Harbor is bombed.

00:45:26.330 --> 00:45:26.810
Japan,

00:45:27.051 --> 00:45:27.911
a German ally,

00:45:28.231 --> 00:45:29.572
attacks the United States.

00:45:30.392 --> 00:45:32.053
It's over for America First.

00:45:32.394 --> 00:45:33.454
The group's dissolved.

00:45:34.255 --> 00:45:36.256
The country's united to enter into the war,

00:45:36.596 --> 00:45:38.517
and Lindbergh's now out of the picture.

00:45:40.998 --> 00:45:43.340
Roosevelt refuses to allow him a post in the army.

00:45:43.940 --> 00:45:46.942
He's muzzled and condemned to obscure technical jobs,

00:45:47.522 --> 00:45:49.663
such as testing these altitude pressure chambers.

00:46:05.936 --> 00:46:07.240
In 1944,

00:46:07.562 --> 00:46:11.173
Lindbergh manages to get the post of civilian consultant in the Pacific.

00:46:12.299 --> 00:46:15.260
His experience allows him to conduct advanced pilot training.

00:46:16.280 --> 00:46:19.301
Flying clandestine combat missions against the Japanese,

00:46:19.641 --> 00:46:23.742
he once again experiences the thrill and craziness of his youthful piloting days.

00:46:24.923 --> 00:46:27.663
He does his part in the war against the Yellow Asiatics,

00:46:28.004 --> 00:46:28.964
not against the Germans,

00:46:29.244 --> 00:46:30.605
but he's fighting for his country.

00:46:31.505 --> 00:46:32.385
He takes risks.

00:46:33.305 --> 00:46:35.086
He flies bombing missions in the islands,

00:46:35.286 --> 00:46:36.606
shoots down Japanese planes.

00:46:37.306 --> 00:46:38.747
He recovers his reputation.

00:46:43.340 --> 00:46:46.501
He's reinstated in the Army with the rank of Brigadier General.

00:46:47.741 --> 00:46:50.862
He's even considered for the office of Secretary of the Air Force,

00:46:51.242 --> 00:46:54.923
but he prefers to stay a simple aeronautic advisor to President Eisenhower.

00:46:56.784 --> 00:46:58.484
He makes frequent trips to Europe,

00:46:58.844 --> 00:47:01.565
like this mission during NATO's very first maneuvers.

00:47:05.786 --> 00:47:07.327
In 1953,

00:47:07.328 --> 00:47:08.947
Lindbergh publishes his memoirs,

00:47:09.608 --> 00:47:10.488
The Spirit of St.

00:47:10.588 --> 00:47:10.928
Louis.

00:47:11.563 --> 00:47:12.845
It becomes a bestseller,

00:47:13.405 --> 00:47:14.706
wins the Pulitzer Prize,

00:47:15.967 --> 00:47:20.990
and is made into a movie directed by Billy Wilder and starring James Stewart.

00:47:28.135 --> 00:47:31.577
Berg's missions to Europe are also practical for his personal life.

00:47:33.619 --> 00:47:35.700
They justify long stays away from home.

00:47:42.556 --> 00:47:43.657
suffers from these trips,

00:47:44.137 --> 00:47:47.459
her writing success doesn't make up for her husband's long absences.

00:47:50.501 --> 00:47:54.883
She'll never learn that Lindbergh has founded three other secret families in Europe.

00:47:56.244 --> 00:47:57.125
Forty years later,

00:47:57.505 --> 00:47:58.786
and after Anne Moreau's death,

00:47:59.406 --> 00:48:02.648
Lindbergh's double or triple life is finally revealed.

00:48:09.956 --> 00:48:12.197
He fathered two children with his secretary,

00:48:12.917 --> 00:48:14.477
three others with one of her friends,

00:48:15.057 --> 00:48:17.198
and two more with this friend's sister,

00:48:21.239 --> 00:48:23.480
making a total of 13 children,

00:48:24.019 --> 00:48:25.980
including those he fathered with Anne Moreau,

00:48:27.181 --> 00:48:29.341
all very handsome and all very bright.

00:48:33.562 --> 00:48:35.383
To travel from one family to another,

00:48:35.883 --> 00:48:38.864
Lindbergh drives thousands of miles through Germany and Switzerland.

00:48:39.179 --> 00:48:40.842
In his modest Volkswagen Beetle.

00:48:51.952 --> 00:48:52.893
In the tiny car,

00:48:53.134 --> 00:48:55.195
like in the cockpit of a laughable spirit of St.

00:48:55.235 --> 00:48:55.915
Louis replica,

00:48:56.436 --> 00:48:57.557
gripping his steering wheel,

00:48:57.958 --> 00:49:00.320
he wonders about his life and times.

00:49:02.782 --> 00:49:03.384
He writes,

00:49:04.607 --> 00:49:07.150
Why not start at zero and remake my life,

00:49:07.650 --> 00:49:09.853
setting course by whatever seems worthwhile,

00:49:10.333 --> 00:49:11.494
discarding the rest?

00:49:14.417 --> 00:49:15.358
As a college youth,

00:49:15.919 --> 00:49:18.282
I thought civilization could never be destroyed again.

00:49:19.243 --> 00:49:20.304
That it was too powerful,

00:49:20.964 --> 00:49:21.685
too universal.

00:49:24.108 --> 00:49:30.052
But after seeing the destruction of high explosive bombs and flying over the atomic bomb city of Hiroshima,

00:49:30.733 --> 00:49:32.774
I realized how vulnerable my profession,

00:49:33.034 --> 00:49:33.635
aviation,

00:49:33.995 --> 00:49:35.036
has made all people.

00:49:36.757 --> 00:49:39.979
I truly wonder if aviation has been a benefit to humanity.

00:49:46.463 --> 00:49:48.025
So Lindbergh seeks escape.

00:49:48.805 --> 00:49:52.247
He becomes a defender of ecology and the planet's preservation.

00:49:54.987 --> 00:49:55.949
These rare images,

00:49:56.250 --> 00:49:57.574
possibly the last of him,

00:49:58.034 --> 00:50:00.000
show Lindbergh with an NGO in the Philippines.

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:02.661
Philippine islands working to save endangered tribes.

00:50:12.182 --> 00:50:14.083
He still held to a last hope.

00:50:15.304 --> 00:50:17.525
He said this hope was in the sky,

00:50:18.144 --> 00:50:19.764
in what man has never touched.

00:50:23.025 --> 00:50:24.005
August 26,

00:50:24.007 --> 00:50:25.467
1974.

00:50:26.367 --> 00:50:29.728
Charles Lindbergh died of cancer at 72 years of age.

00:50:29.927 --> 00:50:30.547
In Hawaii,

00:50:32.389 --> 00:50:34.110
he departed for the Freemasons'

00:50:34.130 --> 00:50:35.150
eternal hereafter,

00:50:35.971 --> 00:50:37.893
seeking the universe's great architect,

00:50:38.572 --> 00:50:42.154
who will undoubtedly show understanding for the lone eagle.

